      </fo:static-content>
      <fo:flow flow-name="xsl-region-body"
               font-size="10.5pt"
               line-height="12.5pt"
               font-family="Ludolfus"
               text-align="justify"
               hyphenate="true"><!-- CATALOGUE     -->
         <fo:block id="BDLaethe37"
                   text-align="center"
                   page-break-inside="avoid"
                   page-break-after="avoid"
                   space-before="3mm">
            <fo:block font-weight="800">Bodleian Aeth. e. 37</fo:block>
            <fo:block>(Juel-Jensen 11)</fo:block>
            <fo:block/>
         </fo:block>
         <fo:block text-align="center" space-before="2mm" space-after="3mm"/>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm" space-after="3mm" white-space-collapse="true">Parchment codex, composite147×101 mm (weight: 326g), 96 fols.  Unit 1:  eighteenth–mid-twentieth century. Unit 2:  eighteenth–mid-twentieth century.</fo:block>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm" white-space-collapse="true">
            <fo:block font-style="italic"
                      space-after="3mm"
                      page-break-inside="avoid"
                      page-break-after="avoid">Binding</fo:block>
                                    The manuscript is bound in wooden boards and covered with  on the spine.
                                
                                    The boards are cut to the same size as the bookblock and have shallow external bevels. The grain runs vertically.
                                    The external surface of the boards is polished, while the inner surface exhibits signs of the working tool.
                                
                                    The sewing is a chainstitch on four sewing stations, broken and repaired with a different kind of thread. Both threads are Z-plied.
                                
                                    The small holes close to the head and tail of the quires probably indicate that quire tackets were originally present.
                                    A sheet of parchment is folded around and sewn with the bookblock. Fol. 1 and fol. 96 are the extensions
                                    of this sort of spine lining. A piece of brown leather has been sewn over it as an additional protection.
                                
                                    The original leather satchel is preserved. It is a one-piece, double-walled satchel where the back panel overlaps over the front.
                                    The satchel is equipped with long flaps extending from the side panels and a main rounded flap extending from the back to the front
                                    panel to cover the opening of the book compartment.
                                    The hanging strap is formed by two strips of leather whose ends are overlapped and stitched together. The strap passes through two flaps
                                    extending from the bottom panel and slides under a leather belt wrapped around all the sides that also keeps the main flap closed.
                                </fo:block>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm" white-space-collapse="true">
            <fo:block font-style="italic"
                      space-after="3mm"
                      page-break-inside="avoid"
                      page-break-after="avoid">Foliation</fo:block>
            <fo:block>Pencil foliation in the upper right corner (1–96).</fo:block>
         </fo:block>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm" white-space-collapse="true">
            <fo:block font-style="italic"
                      space-after="3mm"
                      page-break-inside="avoid"
                      page-break-after="avoid">History</fo:block>
            <fo:block>The provenance of the manuscript is unknown. Possibly, Unit 2  (a single bifolio)
                            originated from another manuscript and was reused as flyleaves
                            at the production of Unit 1. Another possibility
                            is that the text in Unit 2
                            (which forms an integral unit and is not connected to the text in Unit 1)
                            was written on the bifolio after it had been used as flyleaves.
                            It cannot be excluded that this manuscript is identical to the
                            ‘<fo:inline font-style="italic">Deggwa</fo:inline>’ which Juel-Jensen acquired in
                            <fo:inline id="d1e11135LOC4244Laliba">Lālibalā (town)</fo:inline> during his first
                            visit to Ethiopia, starting in February 1973, see
                            Juel-Jensen 1977,  63.
                        </fo:block>
            <fo:block>The manuscript entered the Bodleian Libraries as part of the
                            bequest of <fo:inline id="d1e11145PRS5782JuelJen">Bent Juel-Jensen</fo:inline> after his death in 2006.</fo:block>
         </fo:block>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm">
            <fo:block font-style="italic"
                      space-after="3mm"
                      page-break-inside="avoid"
                      page-break-after="avoid">Bibliography</fo:block>Juel-Jensen 1977.</fo:block>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm" space-after="3mm" white-space-collapse="true"/>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm" space-after="3mm">Unit 1</fo:block>
         <fo:block>Parchment, 92 fols (fols 2–93), eighteenth–mid-twentieth century.</fo:block>
         <fo:block space-before="2mm" white-space-collapse="true">
            <fo:block font-style="italic"
                      space-after="3mm"
                      page-break-inside="avoid"
                      page-break-after="avoid">Main Contents</fo:block>
            <fo:block hyphenate="true"
                      font-family="Ludolfus"
                      space-after="3mm"
                      id="d1e11184LIT2361somade">
               <fo:inline>I) Fols 4ra–93vb: <fo:inline xml:lang="en" hyphenate="true">‘Antiphonary for Lent’ (​ጾመ፡ ​ድጓ፡), CAe 2361.</fo:inline>
               </fo:inline>
            </fo:block>
            <fo:block hyphenate="true"
                      font-family="Ludolfus"
                      space-after="3mm"
                      id="d1e11192LIT2361somade#FirstWeek">
               <fo:inline>I-1) Fols 4ra–19va: <fo:inline xml:lang="en" hyphenate="true">Antiphonary for Lent: Set of antiphons for the first week of the Great Fast (​ዘወ​ረደ፡,
                     Za-warada).</fo:inline>
               </fo:inline>
               <fo:block start-indent="10mm">Incipit: <fo:inline font-family="Ludolfus" letter-spacing="0.5pt" font-size="0.9em">
                                    ​ምስ​ባክ፡ ​ዘድ​ራረ፡ ​ጾም፡ ​በ፪፡ ​ዘወ​ረደ፡ ​እም​ላዕ​ሉ፡ ​አይሁ 
                                    ​ድ፡ ​ሰቀ​ሉ፡ ​ወሚ​መ፡ ​ኢያ​እመ​ሩ፡ ​እግ​ዚአ፡ ​ኵሉ፡ ​ዘየ​ሐዩ፡ ​በቃሉ<fo:inline>፡ (!)</fo:inline>
                  </fo:inline>
               </fo:block>
               <fo:block start-indent="10mm">Explicit: <fo:inline font-family="Ludolfus" letter-spacing="0.5pt" font-size="0.9em">
                                    ​ሰላ፡  ​ሰላ​መ፡ ​አብ፡ ​እስ​መ፡ ​ይቤ፡ ​መስ​ቀል፡ ​ሞዓ፡ ​ሞት፡
                                    ​ተሞ​ዓ፡ ​ኃይ {ለ}<fo:inline>[፡]</fo:inline> ​መስ​ቀሉ፡ ​አብ​ርሃ
                                     ፨
                                </fo:inline>
               </fo:block>
            </fo:block>
